sections.s2_technical:
RabbitMQ is a broker built around exchanges and queues. Producers publish to an exchange, which routes messages to queues based on rules, and consumers acknowledge each message so the broker knows it was handled. It excels at complex routing and per-message workflows, and traditionally deletes a message once acknowledged. It is a good fit when you need rich routing and moderate throughput.

Amazon SQS is a fully managed queue with almost no operational burden. A standard queue gives at-least-once delivery and best-effort ordering with massive scale, while a FIFO queue gives ordering and exactly-once processing within limits. It is ideal when you want a simple, reliable queue without running infrastructure.

Kafka is a distributed commit log. Messages are appended to topics split into partitions, retained for a configurable time regardless of consumption, so consumers can replay history. It is built for very high throughput and for multiple independent consumer groups reading the same stream. It is the right choice for event streaming, analytics pipelines, and event sourcing.

```java
// Kafka: produce to a partition by key so same-key messages stay ordered
producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("transfers", "SAV2001", transferJson));
// Consumer group: each partition read by exactly one consumer in the group
consumer.subscribe(List.of("transfers"));
```

Delivery semantics matter enormously in banking. At-least-once means a message may be redelivered, so consumers must be idempotent. Exactly-once is harder and, in Kafka, achieved with transactional producers and idempotent writes within the Kafka boundary. In practice I design for at-least-once plus idempotent consumers, because that is simpler and robust. Consumer groups give horizontal scaling: a group shares a topic's partitions among its members so each message is processed once per group, and adding members increases parallelism up to the partition count.

A dead-letter queue captures messages that repeatedly fail processing, so a single poison message does not block the queue or get silently lost; it is moved aside for investigation after a retry limit. A senior nuance is that ordering guarantees are per partition, so if you need strict ordering for an account, you partition by account id, accepting that this limits parallelism for a hot account, and you choose the platform by matching its guarantees to the domain rather than by fashion.

